          {
            "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
            "quote": "This triggers the release of calcium ions (Ca ++ ) from storage in the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR).",
            "location": "paragraph_1"
          },
          {
            "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
            "quote": "Ca ++ ions are then pumped back into the SR, which causes the tropomyosin to reshield (or re-cover) the binding sites on the actin strands.",
            "location": "paragraph_3"
          }
        ],
        "cardinality": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "op": "add",
      "collection": "entities",
      "item": {
        "semantic_id": "ENT_SARCOPLASM",
        "scientific_name": "Sarcoplasm (cytosol) of the skeletal muscle fibre",
        "entity_type": "intracellular_compartment",
        "evidence_ids": ["E_d6fd3723dbb7", "E_62c37d99e9ae"],
        "support": [
          {
            "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
            "quote": "As long as Ca ++ ions remain in the sarcoplasm to bind to troponin, which keeps the actin-binding sites “unshielded,” and as long as ATP is available to drive the cross-bridge cycling and the pulling of actin strands by myosin, the muscle fiber will continue to shorten to an anatomical limit.",
            "location": "paragraph_1"
          },
          {
            "evidence_id": "E_62c37d99e9ae",
            "quote": "This can only happen in the presence of calcium, which is kept at extremely low concentrations in the sarcoplasm. If present, calcium ions bind to troponin, causing conformational changes in troponin that allow tropomyosin to move away from the myosin binding sites on actin.",
            "location": "paragraph_26"
          }
        ],
        "cardinality": 1
      },
      "justifies_issue_id": "INDEPENDENT_1"
    },
